lesson physics-reading-a-rate v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=5 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Reading a rate"
  goal "Read a value off a gauge, and use a steady rate to work forwards or backwards in time."

  look {
    scene 26x26 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=start {
        row dials space=2 align=start {
          fuelgauge gauge level=0.625
          thermometer tube from=0 to=40 step=10 value=24 unit="C"
        }
        doubleline pace steps=4 topTo=200 bottomTo=4 topUnit="km" bottomUnit=hours mark=2
      }
    }
    say """
      A rate says how much of one thing goes with one of another: litres a hundred kilometres,
      degrees an hour, kilometres an hour. A gauge shows where you are now; the rate is what lets
      you say where you were, or where you will be.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.fuel-left count=2 seed=5
    practice physics.fuel-used count=2 seed=2
    practice physics.temp-rate count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.rate-when count=2 seed=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.fuel-enough k=3 d=200 easy-k=6 easy-d=150
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.rate-two a=5 s=1 h=3 easy-h=2 hard-a=10 hard-s=3 hard-h=4
  }
  remember {
    say "A rate is an amount for each one of something. Multiply to go forward, divide to come back."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The fuel gauge in the car is the best example there is, because the child can watch it fall over
    a journey whose length they know. Petrol is dollars and cents, so a litre price turns the same
    reading into money. The last question has two things changing at different rates; drawing the
    readings hour by hour in a table is a good way in, and it is how the answer can be checked.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the gauge sits at a quarter or three quarters, the thermometer reads on the numbered marks, and the two thermometers meet after two hours."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the gauge sits at an odd eighth, the readings sit on the half marks, and the two thermometers meet after four hours."
  }
}
